<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bessarion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1403">1403</a>–<a href="/years/1472">1472</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Basilios Bessarion, Cardinal Bessarion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bessarion was a Byzantine scholar and churchman born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>. As metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> he supported the union of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a> in 1439 and was made a cardinal of the Roman church. Settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he promoted Greek learning and gave his great collection of Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, forming the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblioteca-marciana">Biblioteca Marciana</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bessarion.jpg" alt="Bessarion" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodor de Bry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_Bessarion_aport012.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Basilios Bessarion</b>, born in Trebizond around 1403, became a leading theologian, a supporter of the union of the churches at the Council of Florence and a cardinal of the Roman Church, whose library founded the Biblioteca Marciana in Venice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…then Florence in 1438–1439 to reunite the Greek and Latin churches brought Byzantine scholars to Italy: Gemistos Plethon lectured in Florence on Plato, and <b>Bessarion</b>, made a cardinal of the Roman Church in 1439, settled in Italy and later gave his great library of Greek manuscripts to Venice.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Ravenna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1447" aria-label="Trabzon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bessarion"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5972 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5972 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1447">What was here in 1447? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
